Falsifies `capability::subset_matches_allows`.

The defect: `is_subset_of` with its operands the wrong way round, `other.0 & !self.0`
instead of `self.0 & !other.0`. This is the most plausible single defect the function
has, because "is self no more than other" and "does other hold at least self" are the
same sentence read in two directions and the body is four tokens long.

Expected to fail: the `assert_eq!(a.is_subset_of(b), b.allows(a))` in
`subset_matches_allows`. That harness exists precisely to pin the two directions
together, and this is the defect it was written against, so it is the one that must go
red.

**What this record is worth reading for.** Applied to the whole crate, this defect turns
only 2 of the 12 capability harnesses red: this one and
`from_bits_cannot_forge_a_right`. `subset_is_reflexive`, `subset_is_transitive` and
`derive_never_widens_rights` all stay green, the last of them because it states the
property THROUGH the predicate being broken and so cannot see the break.

diff --git a/crates/capability/src/lib.rs b/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
index 33b5819a..21614dfe 100644
--- a/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
@@ -140,7 +140,7 @@ impl Rights {
 
     /// Is `self` no more than `other`? The subset test that [`CapabilityTable::derive`] turns on.
     pub const fn is_subset_of(self, other: Rights) -> bool {
-        self.0 & !other.0 == 0
+        other.0 & !self.0 == 0
     }
 }
 
